1. Introduction
Greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions from human activities into the atmosphere are considered to be the main cause of global warming (Solomon et al., 2007). In 2016, 175 countries signed the Paris Agreement, the main purpose of which is to control the global average temperature rise within 2°C and strive to achieve zero GHG emissions in the second half of the 21st century. China is one of the world's largest carbon-emitting countries. In 2017, total carbon emission in China accounted for about 28.27% of the global total and had not yet reached its peak (International Energy Agency, 2019). In 2020, China proposed to strive to achieve the goal of carbon peak by 2030 and carbon neutrality by 2060. China plans that the energy efficiency of key industries would reach the international advanced level by 2030, and the energy consumption per unit of GDP would be greatly reduced; carbon emissions per unit of GDP decrease by more than 65% compared with 2005; the consumption proportion of non-fossil energy reaches about 25%; and the carbon emissions reach the peak and decreased steadily (The State Council, 2016, 2021). The proportion of non-fossil energy consumption would reach more than 80%, and the goal of carbon neutrality would be successfully achieved by 2060 (The State Council, 2016, 2021). The plastic industry is an industry with high energy consumption and carbon emissions. Plastic products are expected to contribute 15% to global GHG emissions by 2050 (Ellen MacArchur Foundation, 2016). GHG is emitted in the plastics life cycle (including the production (P), processing and manufacturing (PM), recycling, landfill, and incineration) (Hamilton et al., 2019). Plastics can be widely used in various industries (such as packaging and transportation). In recent years, the yield of plastics is amazing, and 8.3 billion tonnes of plastics were produced worldwide (Geyer et al., 2017). The huge production of plastics has brought a lot of GHG emissions. In the United States, the emissions from the exploitation and transportation of fossil fuels (mainly shale gas) related to plastics production were 9.5-10.5 million tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent (CO2e) in 2015 (Hamilton et al., 2019). The research showed that plastic consumption in the United States contributed to the GHG emissions of 104 million metric tons CO2e (Nicholson et al., 2021). By 2030, the net GHG emissions from the incineration of plastic packaging waste would increase to 84 million tonnes (Ellen MacArthur Foundation, 2016b; Walker and McKay, 2021). The processes of production and incineration for plastics contributed more than 850 million tonnes of GHG, equivalent to the GHG emissions of 189 coal-fired power plants (500 megawatts) in 2019 (Hamilton et al., 2019).
At present, GHG emissions associated with plastics have attracted extensive attention. For example, Dormer et al. (2013) carried out cradle-to-grave research on plastic trays, deeply analyzed the carbon footprint of their products.(Zheng and Suh, 2019) analyzed global GHG emissions from both traditional plastics and bioplastics throughout their life cycle. They found that the global GHG emissions of traditional plastics are projected to grow to 650 million tonnes of CO2e by 2050. Gomes et al. (2019) carried out a systematic review on the LCA studies of PET packaging materials.Brizga et al. (2020) analyzed the potential environmental consequences of replacing petrochemical packaging plastics with bioplastics (in terms of GHG emissions, land and water footprints) based on a review of LCA studies and calculations to assess the footprint of such alternatives.Su et al. (2020) quantified the environmental impact (CO2e) of raw material production of express packaging, box manufacturing, and waste management. It showed that there was a great opportunity to reduce the consumption and impact of packaging materials.Meys et al. (2021) constructed a bottom-up model, which studied the pathways to net-zero GHG emissions of 90% global plastics. The results showed that net-zero GHG emissions could be achieved by combining the efficient utilization of carbon dioxide (CO2) and biomass.(Alsabri and Al-Ghamdi, 2020) analyzed the LCA and carbon footprint of the production process of polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyethylene (PE), and polypropylene (PP) plastics to find the environmental impact leading to global warming. It is found that PVC had higher energy consumption and carbon emissions than other plastics. In conclusion, although these studies involve the carbon footprint of plastics, however, there are few studies on life-cycle GHG emissions from the most commonly used polystyrene (PS), PVC, and acrylonitrile-butadiene-styrene (ABS) plastics in China, especially on the carbon peak path and strategy of these plastics in 2030. In addition, most of the previous studies on plastic carbon footprint are based on LCA, which can assess the level of environmental impacts throughout its life cycle (from material generation to waste treatment), which cannot be revealed by material flow analysis (MFA).(García-Guaita et al., 2018) However, the detailed mass flow and balance in the MFA system can provide inventory or activity level for LCA (Allesch and Brunner, 2015; Wang et al., 2022), which will solve the difficulty of obtaining the activity level required for GHG emissions accounting in the past. Therefore, this study will explore the GHG emissions of the plastics life cycle in combination with LCA aCPPIA, 2018nd MFA.
China is the world's largest producer and consumer of plastics (Chen et al., 2019; PlasticsEurope, 2018). PS, PVC, and ABS are the most widely used plastics in China, and the production and consumption of these three plastics reached 24.70 and 26.77 million tonnes respectively in 2017, which accounted for more than half of that of commonly used plastics (Chen et al., 2019;CPCIA, 2018; ). However, the detailed information on the life-cycle GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics, especially the GHG emissions in the production (P), processing and manufacturing (PM), waste management and recycling (WM&R) stages in China, as well as the key path of carbon peak in 2030, is limited. This leads to the fact that the key path of emission reduction for PS, PVC, and ABS plastics in the life cycle under the background of carbon neutralization is unknown, and effective strategies of emission reduction cannot be proposed. To solve this problem, this study combines dynamic MFA with LCA to establish the GHG emissions accounting model of PS, PVC, and ABS in China. According to the model, the GHG emission characteristics of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics from 2007 to 2017 are analyzed, and the carbon peak in 2030 is further explored. This study mainly has two innovations: 1) It reveals the characteristics of GHG emissions from the PS, PVC, and ABS life cycle from the time series; 2) According to the historical emission trend, it further explores the key path and strategy for realizing the carbon peak of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics. This study would clarify the main sources of GHG emissions in the PS, PVC, and ABS plastics life cycle, and put forward policies to improve the GHG emissions from PS, PVC, and ABS plastics, which would provide scientific support for the PS, PVC, and ABS plastics industry to achieve the goal of carbon peak and neutralization.
2. Methodology
2.1. Spatial and temporal scale and extent
The system boundary of this study includes the time and space boundaries. The temporal scale is 2007-2017. The spatial scale is the Chinese mainland (excluding Hong Kong, Macao, and Taiwan). The material flows and GHG accounting system for PS, PVC, and ABS plastics constructed in this study mainly include four stages: P, PM, use, WM&R stages. Our previous work has established the primary material flows model of plastics (Chu et al., 2021a). Based on this model, some revisions are made to make it more suitable for analyzing the GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics in the whole life cycle. See Fig. S1 and supplementary materials (SI, including unit process, system boundary, life cycle inventory, etc.) for details. In this study, GHG emissions from three life cycle stages of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics were considered: first, P stage, including all activities from the cradle of PS, PVC, and ABS to the gate of resin production plant (production, general purpose, high impact, granulate, and bulk polymerization); second, PM stage, covering the manufacturing process of transforming resin into final plastics products (injection molding, blow molding, and extrusion conversion technologies); and finally, WM&R stage, referring to the incineration, landfill, discarded/untreated and recycling process of PS, PVC, and ABS plastic wastes. Due to the unavailability of data, it was not considered the use stage in this study (Fig. 1).
