2.2. Life-cycle GHG emissions of fossil PS, PVC, and ABS plastics
This study combined LCA and MFA methods to analyze the GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS in the whole life cycle. Based on the law of mass balance, an MFA can effectively track the flows of materials in the system and has been widely used in the flows of elements or plastics (Brunner and Rechberger, 2016; Chu et al., 2019; Chu et al., 2021b; Graedel, 2019; Van eygen et al., 2018). When analyzing the flows of PS, PVC, and ABS life cycle, each stage in the system meets the mass balance. We used dynamic MFA to quantify the annual production and the flow and waste volume of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics, which can provide activity level data of each process (technology), to prepare for the accounting of total GHG emissions (Wang et al., 2021). Detailed mass flow and balance in an MFA system can provide an informed inventory or activity level for LCA (Allesch and Brunner, 2015; Wang et al., 2022). Therefore, the material or waste flows calculated by MFA are used as the activity level required to calculate GHG in this study (i.e., the value of Q in formula 3). An LCA is widely used to analyze the environmental impact and GHG emissions of products in the life cycle (Chu et al., 2022, Posen et al., 2017; Wu et al., 2019). According to the provisions of IPCC and other studies, we adopted a process-based approach to quantify the GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics during their life cycle (assuming the sum of the research processes) (IPCC, 2006; Milford et al., 2013; Wang et al., 2021). We calculate the plastic flows of each product based on the top-down method. The detailed calculation formulas of flows in the P process are given below. Detailed formulas for other stages are provided in supplementary materials.
