2.3. Data sources
The data involved in this study are the relevant data required for MFA of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics (such as plastic consumption, consumption structure, plastic waste treatment proportion, etc.), and these data would prepare for the data of accounting activity level. And the emission factor data required for accounting for GHG emissions is needed. By consulting statistical yearbooks, customs statistical information, relevant literature, and industry reports, we can obtain the yield, import, and export volume of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics in the P stage. The proportion and consumption of PS, PVC, and ABS plastic products (such as film, wood goods, sheets, tubes, profiles & components (STP&C), foam plastics, etc.) in the PM stage and the proportion and amount of plastic waste incinerated, landfilled, recycled, untreated/discarded in the WM&R stage are obtained (Chu et al., 2021, CNTIF, 2008-2018; CPCIA, 2008-2018; CPPIA, 2008-2018; Jiang et al., 2020; SFI, 2019). Emission factor data are mainly from the Chinese life cycle database (CLCD), ecoinvent 3.4 database, literature, and European life cycle database (CLCD; European Commission & JRC, 2018; Wernet et al., 2016; Zheng and Suh, 2019). Due to the lack of some emission factor data for PS, PVC, and ABS plastics in China, we can only estimate based on European database. Due to the unavailability of data, uncertainty is inevitable in this study. This study analyzed the effect of uncertainty on results in the supplementary materials. See Table S1-S7, S12 for detailed data sources and uncertainty analysis.
2.4. Scenario analysis of carbon peak in 2030
In October 2021, China issued a guideline to achieve carbon peak and neutrality, requiring that by 2025, an economic system of green and low-carbon circular development would be initially completed, and the energy utilization efficiency of key industries would be greatly improved. The CO2 emission per unit of GDP would be 18% in 2025 lower than that in 2020, and the consumption proportion of non-fossil energy would reach about 20% in 2025, laying a solid foundation for achieving carbon peak and carbon neutralization (The State Council, 2021). Based on this opinion, this study sets up a scenario analysis of energy structure and plastics consumption transformation, focusing on the carbon peak of the PS, PVC, and ABS plastics in the life cycle in 2030. The first is the baseline scenario, which represents the development trend of energy structure and plastic consumption structure without any human policy interference according to the current trend of development. There are three scenarios of energy structure transformation and plastic consumption change. In particular, given the guidance's strong mandate, changes in the energy mix need to be considered in all three scenarios. That is to explore the impact of the change of plastic consumption on the realization of the carbon peak in the case that the energy structure target is bound to be achieved. Scenario 1 is that the proportion of non-fossil energy consumption will reach 20% in 2025, the consumption of plastics remains unchanged; scenario 2 shows that the proportion of fossil energy is about 80% in 2025, and the consumption of plastics remains declining; scenario 3 shows that in 2025, the proportion of non-fossil energy is about 20%, and the consumption of plastics remains rising slowly. See Table S8 for the specific scenario explanation.
3. Results and Discussion
3.1. Characteristics of cumulative GHG emissions in the PS, PVC, and ABS life cycle in 2007 - 2017
A material metabolism model was established to show the mass flow of PS, PVC, and ABS from production to disposal, and provide activity level data required for GHG emissions (Fig. 2). Detailed data and calculations were provided in the supplementary materials. Based on the results, the life cycle GHG emissions of these plastics were further analyzed. The study found that the cumulative GHG emissions from PS, PVC, and ABS in the P stage were 163.58, 492.99, and 87.60 Tg CO2e respectively from 2007 to 2017, accounting for 71.0%, 72.8%, and 61.2% of the total emissions respectively (Fig. 3). The main source of GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics life cycle is the P stage. The GHG emissions from the PM stage are second only to those in the P stage, with 45.37, 123.58, and 37.26 Tg CO2e respectively, accounting for 19.7%, 16.8%, and 26.0% of the total emissions in the whole life cycle. It is worth noting that the key paths of GHG emissions in the PM stage are different for PS, PVC, and ABS plastics. For example, for PS plastics, the manufacturing processes of PS STP&C and foam plastics lead to a large amount of GHG, and their emissions account for 39.1% and 32.2% of the emissions in the PM stage, reaching 17.76 and 14.63 Tg CO2e respectively (Fig. 4). Different from PS plastics, the GHG emissions from the manufacturing process of PVC film and STP&C plastics are the main contributions of the PM stage of PVC, with their emissions reaching 19.34 and 45.97 Tg CO2e respectively, accounting for 15.7% and 37.2% in this stage. It is worth noting that the manufacturing process of PVC used for others also produces a large amount of GHG, with their emissions reaching 33.12 Tg CO2e. Similar to PS and PVC plastics, the manufacturing process of ABS plastics STP&C contributes the main GHG in this stage. Notably, its emissions account for 84.3% of the emissions in the PM stage. Therefore, the manufacturing processes of PS STP&C, PS foam plastics, PVC film, PVC STP&C, and ABS STP&C are the main contributor to GHG emissions in the PM process, and we suggest that strict reduction policies of GHG emissions should be adopted for the manufacturing process of these products. Nicholson et al. (2021) found that 48 MT CO2e is produced in the production process during the annual plastic life cycle in the US. In contrast, China produced 121.08 Tg CO2e in the plastic P stage in 2017. This may be due to the difference in energy structure between China and the US. China has a higher proportion in coal than the US, while the US has a higher proportion in natural gas and nuclear energy Liu et al., 2009, International Energy Agency 2020).
