3.2. Characteristics of GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS from 2007 to 2017
The life-cycle GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS in China from 2007 to 2017 are shown in Fig. 5(a). In 2007-2017, the GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS in the whole life cycle were 230.34, 677.43, 143.12 Tg CO2e respectively. In comparison, the PVC life cycle produced the largest GHG emissions in this period. Life-cycle GHG emissions of PS showed a trend of slowly increasing at first and then slowly decreasing (except 2009) from 2007 to 2017. From 2007 to 2014, the GHG emissions of PS increased slowly with an average growth rate of 8.8%, reaching the maximum value (26.93 Tg CO2e) in 2014. However, it decreased with an average reduction rate of 9.7% after 2014, and the emissions reached 15.94 Tg CO2e by 2017. In terms of life cycle stages, the GHG emissions from the PS P stage showed a trend of the first decline, then rise, and then decline from 2007 to 2017. From 2007 to 2009, GHG emissions in the P stage decreased from 11.06 to 9.70 Tg CO2e. This should be due to the decline in the yield of PS plastics due to the impact of the financial crisis. GHG emissions in the PS P stage showed a slow rise from 2009 to 2016. It is worth noting that its growth rate gradually decreased from 28.8% in 2009 to 1.9% in 2016. This shows that although the GHG emissions in the P stage are rising, their growth is becoming slower and slower. Until 2017, it showed a downward trend, which may be affected by the ban on limiting plastic production. This shows that one of the potentials for carbon neutralization in the PS P stage is to reduce unnecessary production in this stage. For the PS PM stage, the GHG emissions in the STP&C PM stage did not fluctuate significantly from 2007 to 2017, basically remained within the range of 1.1-2.0 Tg CO2e, and decreased significantly in 2017, which was also affected by plastic limit order (Fig. 5). Similar to PS STP&C, the GHG emissions of PS foam plastics and other products in the PM stage did not fluctuate greatly from 2007 to 2017. However, it decreased significantly in 2009 and 2017. In terms of the time series for the WM&R stage, GHG emissions from landfills increased at an average rate of 20.9% from 2007 to 2017 (except for 2009 and 2017), with a particularly large increase from 2009 to 2010 (Fig. 6). Different from the landfill process, GHG emissions from the incineration process showed an upward trend (except 2009), with an average increase rate of 47.3% from 2007 to 2017. Despite the impact of the plastic restriction order in 2017, which reduced the total amount of waste, the amount of waste incinerated increased, so the emissions from 2016 to 2017 were still rising. From 2007 to 2017, the emissions from the recycling process showed a rapid increase after 2009 and then a slow increase. The possible GHG brought by untreated plastics wastes showed a gradual downward trend from 2007 to 2017, which should be that with the improvement of the waste management system, untreated plastic wastes were gradually incinerated or landfilled.
