Different from PS, the life-cycle GHG emissions of PVC from 2007 to 2017 showed a trend of slow decline at first and then slow rise. From 2007 to 2008, the GHG emission of PVC in the whole life cycle decreased from 43.77 Tg CO2e to 39.69 Tg CO2e, but after 2008, it increased at an average increase rate of 9.2%, and by 2017, the emission reached 85.42 Tg CO2e. The emission characteristics of each stage of PVC are different from those of PS from 2007 to 2017. In addition to 2008, the GHG emissions in the PVC P stage showed an upward trend at an average growth rate of 8.4% after 2009, and the GHG emissions also increased from 30.52 Tg CO2e in 2009 to 59.64 Tg CO2e in 2017. In the PM stage, the GHG emissions from the manufacturing of PVC film, synthetic leather, STP&C, cables, and other products during this period reached lowest point in 2008 and then showed a large increase until 2013. However, since 2014, although it was also increasing, the growth rate was not large. These characteristics are inconsistent with the PS plastics PM stage. In the stage of WM&R, the GHG emissions from the processes of the landfill, incineration, recycling, and untreated/discarded plastics were underestimated in 2008 and increased rapidly in 2010 and 2017. And it reached 0.73, 6.49, 4.03, and 0.20 Tg CO2e respectively by 2017. Different from landfill, incineration and recycling, GHG emissions from untreated plastic wastes decreased in 2010, 2015, and 2017.
The trend of life-cycle GHG emission of ABS is very different from that of PS and PVC. From 2007 to 2017, the GHG emissions of ABS kept a steady upward trend, from 8.71 Tg CO2e in 2007 to 19.72 Tg CO2e in 2017, with an average annual growth rate of 8.7%. The emission characteristics of the ABS life cycle are different from PS and PVC from 2007 to 2017. In the P stage, GHG emissions showed an upward trend with an average growth rate of 9.9%, and the amount of GHG also increased from 4.76 Tg CO2e in 2007 to 11.97 Tg CO2e in 2017. During the PM stage, the GHG emissions from the manufacturing of ABS STP&C and other products increased rapidly in 2014. In the WM&R stage, the GHG emissions in the processes of the landfill, incineration, recycling, and untreated in this period reached a low point in 2012 as a result of policy interventions.
3.3. Scenario analysis of carbon peak in 2030
Under the baseline scenario, the GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics does not reach the carbon peak in 2030 and may reach 23.17, 146.41, and 42.31 Tg CO2e respectively in 2030 (Fig. 7), which will be 1.5, 1.7, and 2.1 times that in 2017. Under the scenario of changing the energy structure and plastics production, in scenario 1, the GHG emissions of PS will achieve a carbon peak before 2030 and achieve a carbon emission reduction of 3.45 Tg CO2e in 2035 compared with 2017. Compared with the baseline scenario, the GHG emissions of scenario 1 in 2030 and 2035 are reduced by 39% and 53% respectively. Scenario 2 has greater potential for carbon emission reduction. Since 2030, GHG emissions show a rapid downward trend (Fig. 8). By 2035, GHG emissions will be reduced by 8.47 Tg CO2e compared with 2017. Moreover, GHG emissions in 2030 and 2035 are reduced by 38% and 72% respectively compared with the baseline scenario. In scenario 3, carbon peak can also be achieved before 2030, and GHG emissions will remain constant after 2030. Compared with the baseline scenario, GHG emissions in scenario 3 will be reduced by 38% and 47% in 2030 and 2035, respectively. Similar to PS plastics, under the scenario of changing the energy structure and plastics yield, in scenario 1, the GHG emissions for PVC will achieve a carbon peak before 2030, and a carbon emission reduction of 6.39 Tg CO2e in 2035 compared with 2017. Moreover, GHG emissions in 2035 under scenario 1 are reduced by 56% compared with the baseline scenario. The carbon emission reduction potential in scenario 2 is the most significant (Fig. 8). By 2035, the GHG emissions will be reduced by 38.24 Tg CO2e compared with that in 2017, which is 6 times that in scenario 1. It is worth noting that GHG emissions in 2030 and 2035 under scenario 2 are reduced by 38% and 74% respectively compared with the baseline scenario. The carbon peak also been achieved in scenario 3, but the emission reduction is not as significant as that in scenarios 1 and 2. Under scenario 3, GHG emissions in 2035 are reduced by 50% compared with the baseline scenario. Similarly, the GHG emissions of ABS will achieve the carbon peak goal before 2030, and the emission at the carbon peak is about 27.35 Tg CO2e in scenario 1. Scenario 2 has the most significant carbon emission reduction potential (Fig. 8). Similar to PS and PVC, ABS plastics in scenario 3 also achieve a carbon peak. Moreover, compared with the baseline scenario, GHG emissions in 2035 under scenarios 1, 2 and 3 are reduced by 61%, 76% and 48% respectively. The analysis shows that the goal of carbon neutralization is also most likely to be achieved under the change of energy structure and plastic yield.
