4. Discussion
The P stage is the main source of GHG in the whole life cycle of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics. The PM stage is second only to the P stage, and relatively speaking, the emissions in the WM&R stage are the lowest. Similarly, the production of raw materials (68.4%) is the main stage of environmental impact in the life cycle of express packaging materials, followed by the manufacturing process (18.8%) and the end of life stage (12.7%) (Su et al., 2020). Zheng and Suh, (2019) found that most of the GHG emissions were generated in the plastic production stage, accounting for 61% of the total emissions, followed by the PM stage, accounting for 30%, accounting for 91% in total. This is similar to the results of this study. The P stage and PM stage are also the main emission processes in this study, accounting for 89%. They also found that the global petroleum-based plastics (including 10 kinds) emitted 1.8 GtCO2e over their life cycle in 2015. We found that China's petroleum-based PS, PVC, and ABS plastics emitted 121.08 TgCO2e over their life cycle in 2017. Therefore, only these three plastics in China emitted 7% of the global GHG. Nowadays, the demand for plastics is large, and the production of a large number of plastics requires a lot of energy input. The production of polymers consumes a lot of fossil fuel energy during crude oil extraction and refining. GHG sources include direct emissions (such as methane leakage and combustion) from fuel combustion and energy consumption during oil and gas drilling. The manufacturing process of plastic products is energy-intensive and emission-intensive. By cracking alkanes into olefins, polymerizing and plasticizing olefins into plastic resins, and other chemical refining processes, A lot of GHG are generated (Cai et al., 2019;Hamilton et al., 2019). This study also found that, in contrast, the PVC life cycle generated the largest GHG emissions from 2007 to 2017. This is similar to the results of Alsabri and Al-Ghamdi (2020), who show that PVC has higher energy consumption and carbon emission than other plastics.
To reduce GHG emissions, it seems feasible to replace petroleum plastics with bioplastics (Posen et al., 2016;. Semba et al. 2018) found that 20% of sugarcane and 80% of corn produce biological p-xylene, and the GHG emissions of 100% biological derived PET are 24.0% lower than that of petroleum PET. It is worth noting that when considering the landfill and compost emissions of PLA in the waste management stage, its life cycle emissions increase significantly (Benavides et al., 2020). By studying the GHG emissions characteristics of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics on a time scale, it can be found that the GHG emissions increased significantly with the growth of the production of these plastics from 2007 to 2017. Reducing the yield and use of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics is a strong strategy to deal with the climate impact of the whole life cycle of plastics. We recommend some measures to reduce or prohibit the use of non-essential PS, PVC, and ABS plastics products, such as restricting or prohibiting disposable PS, PVC, and ABS plastics (such as PS foam plastics, PVC film, and disposable plastics commonly used in packaging services). At present, China has promulgated the plastics restriction order. Adhering to the plastics restriction order policy will play an important role in promoting the carbon emission reduction of the plastic industry. Stopping the production of petrochemical plastics and the exploitation of fossil fuels are the most effective ways to mitigate the impact on the climate. These solutions need the urgent support of policymakers and charitable funders (Hamilton et al., 2019; Shen et al., 2020). Notably, our research found that the PM sectors of PS STP&C, PS foam plastics, PVC film, PVC STP&C, and ABS STP&C are the key sectors with high GHG emissions. Therefore, we need to take strict control measures for these key sectors, focusing on reducing the production of non-essential products in these plastic products and replacing them with bioplastics. We suggest investment in low-carbon energy and high-efficiency technologies in the PM process of these products, and elimination of technologies and processes with low efficiency, high energy consumption, and high emissions. In the WM&R stage, incineration is the main contribution of GHG. Shen et al. (2020) also found that the incineration of waste plastics will become the main source of GHG emissions. Research showed that each tonne of plastic packaging waste would release 790 kg or about 2.9 tonnes of CO2 into the atmosphere (Hamilton et al., 2019). Hamilton et al. (2019) reported that the net GHG emissions from plastic waste incineration in 2015 were about 16 million tonnes. In 2015, the CO2 emission from waste incineration in the US was 11 million tonnes, and the GHG generated by plastic waste incineration was equivalent to 1.26 million vehicles driving for a year or consuming more than 5 billion gallons of gasoline (US EPA, 2018; Shen et al., 2020). This study showed that in 2017, the GHG generated by the incineration of PS, PVC, and ABS plastic waste would be 9.76 Tg, accounting for 61% and 89% of the above values respectively. Secondly, the recycling process will also produce a large amount of GHG. This is not only a large amount of recovery of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics but also a large amount of fossil energy that needs to be invested in the process of recovery. It is found that since the raw materials of plastic products come from fossil fuels, recycling and reusing plastic wastes will not only reduce the input of fossil fuels but also reduce the related GHG emissions (Liu et al., 2021). Compared with the original plastic products, 130,000 kJ of energy can be saved per kilogram of recycled plastics (Rahimi and García, 2017). Another study shows that recycled plastic materials can replace raw materials and reduce the impact of materials on the environment by 30% (Galve et al., 2021). In general, compared with incineration, recycling is more conducive to emission reduction, but the GHG emissions in the recycling process cannot be ignored. The recovered 3.17 million tonnes of plastic waste can save about 3.2 million tonnes of CO2e (US EPA, 2016). However, the recycling work still needs to solve some challenges (i.e., low-grade plastic waste similar to multi-layer plastic packaging is difficult to separate and dispose, and colorants, fillers, and additives used in the plastic production process affect recycling, and the recycling cost is high), to obtain the GHG benefits brought by recycling in the long run. In addition, biomass utilization, and carbon capture and utilization technologies have significant effects on reducing GHG emissions during the life cycle of plastics. Meys et al. (2021) found that by combining biomass utilization, recycling, and carbon capture and utilization, compared with the current fossil-based production technology, it can not only achieve net-zero GHG emissions of plastics but reduce energy demand and operation cost.
We found that if according to the current development trend, the GHG emissions from the life cycle of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics will not reach the carbon peak goal in 2030. However, under the scenario of changing the energy structure and plastics production, the GHG in the life cycle of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics has reached the carbon peak before 2030. Zheng and Suh, (2019) found that GHG emissions reduction in the plastic life cycle showed great potential under the scenario of energy decarbonization. By 2050, switching to renewable energy sources will reduce GHG emissions by an average of 62%. Our research showed that by 2030, the optimal scenario of changing energy structure and production will reduce GHG emissions by 38% compared with the baseline scenario. Therefore, we must reduce the use of fossil energy, increase the consumption proportion of non-fossil energy (at least 20% in 2025), and limit the consumption of unnecessary plastics (the growth rate of plastics will remain unchanged or decrease by 10% year by year after 2030), which has a very significant benefit for the plastic industry to achieve carbon peak. The lack of this study is mainly due to the unavailability of data, the GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS plastics after 2017 have not been analyzed, and the GHG emissions in the use stage of plastics have not been considered. By improving the database, exploring the GHG emissions in the use stage of plastics after 2017 will be the future research direction. In addition, the GHG emissions generated by discarded waste and plastics in the use phase are also one of the future research directions.
5. Conclusions
This study combines dynamic MFA with LCA to establish a time series analysis model for life-cycle GHG emissions from PS, PVC, and ABS plastics in China. Further, the paths and strategies of carbon peak in 2030 are analyzed. The main conclusions are as follows:
•
The main sources of life-cycle GHG for PS, PVC, and ABS are the P and PM stages. The key paths of GHG emissions are the PM processes of PS STP&C, PS foam plastics, PVC film, PVC STP&C, and ABS STP&C. Therefore, the manufacturing of these plastics requires stricter policies (e.g., limiting disposable PS foam and PVC film) to reduce emissions.
•
The GHG emissions from incineration and recycling cannot be ignored. The cumulative GHG emissions of PS, PVC, and ABS reached 230.34, 677.43, 143.12 Tg CO2e in 2007-2017, respectively. GHG emissions of PS showed a trend of slow rise and then slow decline from 2007 to 2017. Unlike PS, the emissions of ABS maintained a steady upward trend.
•
Under the baseline scenario, the life-cycle GHG of PS, PVC, and ABS could not reach the carbon peak in 2030. Increasing the proportion of non-fossil energy (at least 20% in 2025) and limiting the growth rate of plastics (it remains unchanged or decrease by 10%) will help to reach peak carbon before 2030, and the optimal scenario will reduce GHG emissions by 38% in 2030.
•
According to the results, this study identifies the major sources of life-cycle GHG emissions from PS, PVC, and ABS plastics, and reveals the paths and strategies for carbon peak by 2030.
